<p>Dubai and Abu Dhabi have reopened for a limited number of departures and arrivals after widespread suspensions left travelers stranded, with Emirates and flydubai restarting a small schedule from Monday night. Disruptions are still severe as security concerns and airspace restrictions continue to snarl operations across major regional hubs. The travel crunch followed a rapid escalation after U.S. and Israeli strikes on Iran and subsequent retaliatory attacks affecting Israel and Gulf states, prompting sweeping airspace closures and cancellations. The shock is hitting tourists, migrant workers, business travelers, and religious pilgrims trying to get home or reach their destinations as airlines cut and reroute services. Governments and carriers have started limited evacuation and repatriation efforts where possible, but closed or intermittently restricted airspace over parts of the region keeps the outlook uncertain. Even so, the return of a small number of flights gives stranded passengers a narrow path forward as airports and airlines rebuild schedules.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/EZOqy7MkbVnNlGsVOVxJ6scTvKFrXT-JIDoYQ0bpBdYIWjZabfj9YOb7o1qL0-W3_Z-jhEzeCX1bMNEJ7z90XZSGmd3Uy6dsFdybQqD2nxH_087HNzaq9VZOnEAFEYOEE0jlzBOxuAw4yi-iDBrfNYi5kEnojP8fwODY-dBNCGLospbTS_QXceiQMEGPxqM' alt='Passengers at an airport in the UAE amid rolling flight disruptions.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Delhi disruptions: Delhi airport saw about 80 international flight cancellations on Tuesday amid West Asia tensions, even as some Middle East operations partially resumed.</li><li>Germany’s hurdles: Germany’s evacuation planning hit practical limits, including a flight that left Abu Dhabi for Munich without passengers because it did not have enough cabin crew.</li><li>Hong Kong impact: Hong Kong–Middle East connections also took a hit, with 54 flights disrupted as airspace closures forced tour suspensions until at least next week, according to the South China Morning Post’s check of schedules.</li><li>Global spillover: Airspace disruptions linked to the Iran conflict spread beyond the region, feeding wider global flight cancellations and reroutings reported by broadcasters tracking the situation.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Dubai airport authority: Dubai says it has cleared only a limited number of flights as operations gradually resume for stranded passengers. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/3/3/uae-resumes-limited-flights-amid-travel-chaos-across-middle-east'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>U.S. State Department: The department urged U.S. citizens in 13 countries, including the UAE, Jordan, Saudi Arabia, Egypt, Lebanon and Oman, to “depart now via commercial means due to serious safety risks,” even as cancellations reduced options. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/some-flights-as-governments-seek-to-extract-citizens/'>Michael West</a>)</li><li>Emirates and flydubai: The airlines say they will run a small number of flights to and from Dubai starting Monday night after earlier shutdowns tied to regional airstrikes. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/02/world/middleeast/dubai-flights.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Government of Germany: German authorities are trying to bring home stranded citizens but are running into practical constraints, including crew availability. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/germany-scrambles-to-rescue-thousands-of-citizens-stranded-in-the-middle-east'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Air India: Air India operated a special Dubai–Delhi service that landed as part of efforts to bring home stranded passengers. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/dubai-abu-dhabi-kuwait-middle-east-news-live-updates-flights-cancellations-uae-missile-strikes-airspace-closed-airport-fire-us-issues-depart-now-alert-for-more-than-a-dozen-middle-e-liveblog-13985612.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/some-flights-as-governm